{"cases": [{"target_alias": "SRC_001", "attack": true, "case": "The abstraction is unsound because it maps a structural requirement (separate engines with genuinely different priors) onto a purely behavioral one (divergent latent trajectories within the same model), which is not an abstraction but a category change. The stated refutation condition does not actually protect against this: a stylistic divergence is just one easy failure mode, but the deeper fault is that a single model's latent trajectories remain drawn from the same training distribution and inductive biases regardless of prompting, so 'divergent latent trajectories' can never substitute for genuinely independent priors. The target conflates surface-level behavioral variance with architectural independence.", "counterexample": ["Two model architectures with disjoint training data and different inductive priors identify a logical contradiction", "A single model prompted adversarially produces divergent latent trajectories but, sharing the same training distribution and biases as its generative mode, systematically misses that same logical contradiction", "The 'abstraction' thus silently drops the independence-of-priors requirement that the original separate-engines condition was specifically designed to enforce"]}, {"target_alias": "SRC_002", "attack": true, "case": "The stated reduction mis-identifies what 'separate engines' encodes. Separate engines are required not merely for uncorrelated error terms but for uncorrelated *systematic biases* — errors driven by the underlying architecture, training distribution, and fixed inductive priors. A single engine switching between generative and adversarial modes can only produce decorrelated error terms (different failure frequencies), not decorrelated biases (different *kinds* of failures). By defining the refutation condition as a measured delta in error *detection*, the target allows a mode switch that merely shifts the threshold of detection while leaving the same systematic blind spots intact — a result that would pass the stated refutation but fail the actual purpose of separate engines.", "counterexample": [{"single_engine_generative_mode": "misses logical contradiction X due to a systematic architectural blind spot", "single_engine_adversarial_mode": "also misses logical contradiction X because the same architectural blind spot persists; detects a different, orthogonal error Y instead", "delta_in_error_detection": "positive (satisfies the refutation condition as stated)", "actual_independence_requirement": "violated, because the original systematic bias causing the miss of contradiction X is unchanged"}, {"two_distinct_architectures": "architecture A misses contradiction X but architecture B, having different priors, catches it", "true_reduction_requirement": "met, because the systematic bias itself is decorrelated"}]}]}